// @test regression/INV160-qualified-tuple-destructuring
// @description Pine has no QUALIFIED tuple destructuring. TV reads the qualifier as the variable NAME and answers `""var"" cannot be used as a variable or function name.` at the keyword - ONE error, and none on the names (probed for both `var` and `varip`, 2026-08-25). WHAT THIS FIXTURE CANNOT ASSERT: the other half of the fix is that the parser recovers THROUGH the destructuring so the names stay declared, killing a two-record undefined-variable cascade the CLI used to emit. That cascade is a VALIDATION-channel effect, and this harness skips validation entirely once parsing fails, so an `errors: 0` directive here would be vacuous - it can never go red. The cascade half is verified by the CLI instead, via probes/qualified-destructuring.pine. `const` is here too but with a DIFFERENT message, which is the point of including it: TV reads `const [` as a type annotation rather than a qualifier, so it fails at the first NAME with `Mismatched input "e" expecting set "]"` at that name's own column, never mentioning the keyword. Both wordings are probed and neither may borrow the other's. See INV160.
// @expects parse: fail
// @expects error: line=7, message=/""var"" cannot be used as a variable or function name\./
// @expects error: line=8, message=/""varip"" cannot be used as a variable or function name\./
// @expects error: line=9, column=8, message=/Mismatched input "e" expecting set "\]"/
//@version=6
indicator("qualified tuple destructuring")

f() =>
    [1, 2]

var [a, b] = f()
varip [c, d] = f()
const [e, g] = f()

// The names must still be declared - a missing recovery shows up here as
// undefined-variable errors, not at the declarations above.
plot(a + b + c + d + e + g)
